  "result": "Subcommands now read their defaults from their own entry in `default_map`, so `{\"runserver\": {\"port\": 5000}}` applies to the `runserver` command. The full test suite passes (1907 passed, 102 skipped, 1 xfailed).\n\nThe fix is one line in `src/click/core.py:392`. The child context used to receive the parent's whole `default_map`. It now receives `parent.default_map.get(info_name)`, so a subcommand sees only its own nested dict. When there's no entry for it, the result is `None`, as before.\n\nI made three additions:\n- **Regression test** in `tests/test_defaults.py`. It checks that the nested default applies, that a CLI argument overrides it, and that a missing key falls back to the option's own default.\n- **Changelog entry** under a new \"Unreleased\" heading at the top of `CHANGES.md`.\n- **Nothing committed.** The changes are in the working tree only.\n\nI dropped the `UNSET` handling from the plan. `UNSET` marks individual parameter values, not whole nested maps, so that branch didn't apply.",
